<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is permitted, and nothing narrows it.

  §8 originally required https-only with a developer setting for a local model
  server. That was amended: the app does not inspect the address the user
  supplies, on the grounds that if the server answers, that is the answer. There
  is no EndpointPolicy any more, and no scheme check anywhere.

  The cost, recorded here because this file is where someone will look: an API
  key sent to an `http://` endpoint is readable by anything on the path between
  the phone and that server, and the app will not say so.

  What is still deliberate is the trust anchors below. User-installed
  certificates are not trusted — the default above API 24, restated so that a
  later edit has to remove a line rather than forget one. Without it, a
  self-signed local server would become a supported setup, which is the other
  way people ask for LAN support and a considerably worse one.

  NetworkBoundaryTest asserts this file still permits cleartext, so that
  reversing the decision fails a test rather than quietly breaking a local
  model server on the user's own network.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
